DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These system calls deal with  per‐thread  robust  futex  lists.   These
       lists  are managed in user space: the kernel knows only about the loca‐
       tion of the head of the list.  A thread can inform the  kernel  of  the
       location of its robust futex list using sseett__rroobbuusstt__lliisstt().  The address
       of  a  thread’s  robust  futex  list  can  be  obtained  using  ggeett__rroo‐‐
       bbuusstt__lliisstt().

       The purpose of the robust futex list is to ensure that if a thread  ac‐
       cidentally  fails  to  unlock a futex before terminating or calling eexx‐‐
       eeccvvee(2), another thread that is waiting on that futex is notified  that
       the  former owner of the futex has died.  This notification consists of
       two pieces: the FFUUTTEEXX__OOWWNNEERR__DDIIEEDD bit is set in the futex word, and  the
       kernel  performs  a ffuutteexx(2) FFUUTTEEXX__WWAAKKEE operation on one of the threads
       waiting on the futex.

       The ggeett__rroobbuusstt__lliisstt() system call returns the head of the robust  futex
       list  of  the thread whose thread ID is specified in _p_i_d.  If _p_i_d is 0,
       the head of the list for the calling thread is returned.  The list head
       is stored in the location pointed to by _h_e_a_d___p_t_r.  The size of the  ob‐
       ject pointed to by _*_*_h_e_a_d___p_t_r is stored in _s_i_z_e_p.

       Permission  to  employ ggeett__rroobbuusstt__lliisstt() is governed by a ptrace access
       mode PPTTRRAACCEE__MMOODDEE__RREEAADD__RREEAALLCCRREEDDSS check; see ppttrraaccee(2).

       The sseett__rroobbuusstt__lliisstt() system call requests the  kernel  to  record  the
       head  of  the  list of robust futexes owned by the calling thread.  The
       _h_e_a_d argument is the list head to record.  The _s_i_z_e argument should  be
       _s_i_z_e_o_f_(_*_h_e_a_d_).

